Judgment is self-reported by the sub-agent doing the resolution. There
is no independent check that a resolution marked mechanical actually
was; a rushed or overconfident sub-agent could mislabel a judgment call
and slip past the caller's pause gate. This has not been run through the
skill's own eval loop, so I don't have measured evidence for how
reliably a sub-agent fills this in correctly in practice, only the
reasoning above.

I considered tying Judgment to conflict type directly instead of leaving
it a free assessment (structural implies mechanical, content implies
interpretive), but that just re-introduces the conflation this change is
trying to remove. A modify/delete can need real judgment if the deleted
side's function has to be ported somewhere, and a content conflict can
be a trivial whitespace or version-bump collision.
